course: dictionary
lesson: 844
title: Besides: more or except?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Besides is a small word with a few jobs. Most often it means as well as or in addition to, so it adds one more thing. «He has nine hundred and ninety-nine wives besides Balkis» means Balkis is one more, on top of all the others.
explain: In a sentence with a no word, like nobody or nothing, or in a question, besides means except or apart from. «Nobody besides Tom came» means only Tom came. Test it by swapping in except: Nobody except Tom came. It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: At the start of a sentence, besides means also, and here is another reason. In the story, the trunk never got shorter, «and, besides, it made him squint». The word besides adds a second reason, so use it when you want to give more reasons in your writing.
fact: Besides means as well as, or in addition to.
fact: In sentences with no or in questions, besides means except or apart from.
fact: At the start of a sentence, besides means also, and here is another reason.
fact: «Besides that» and «and besides» are useful phrases for adding reasons.
fact: In maths word problems, besides tells you to add something on.
exercise: «He has nine hundred and ninety-nine wives besides Balkis.» What does besides mean here? | *As well as, in addition to Balkis | Far away from Balkis | Instead of Balkis | Before Balkis
exercise: «Nobody besides Tom came to the party.» What does besides mean in this sentence? | Before | Also, and here is another reason | As well as, in addition to | *Except, apart from
exercise: What does besides mean at the start of a sentence? | *Also, and here is another reason | Except, apart from | Underneath, below | Very quickly
test: «We bought apples, besides pears, bananas and grapes.» What does besides mean here? | Under | Quickly | Except | *In addition to
test: Which meaning of besides fits «Nobody besides Tom came»? | Before | *Except, apart from | As well as | Also, and here is another reason
exam: «The path was long, and besides, it was raining.» Which meaning fits here, and why? | Under the path | *Also, another reason, because the sentence adds a second reason | Except, apart from, because nobody is missing | Before the rain
